{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"MECHANISM VERIFIED IN SOURCE — I filed this on the research agent's assertion without checking it\nmyself. Checking it changes the analysis in two ways, one of which cuts AGAINST the recommendation.\n\nTHE PATH IS REAL: dispatch_one.py:320-326. Identity writes (add_label, set_entity_property) inside a\ntry; 'except Exception as write_err' -> client.delete_issue(jira_key) -> BRIDGE_ALERT -> re-raise.\n\nWHY THE LIVE CLOUD BRIDGE IS NOT HIT — asked directly, and the answer is NOT that Cloud is immune:\n * the trigger is a PERMANENT identity-write failure. The code already wraps both writes in\n   _call_with_retry specifically so transient 5xx/429 do NOT trigger it — the comment says so:\n   'Without this, a single transient failure here triggers the unnecessary rollback branch';\n * on the Cloud project, 'labels' is on the create/edit screens, so add_label succeeds;\n * set_entity_property is a REST properties call and does not go through screen configuration at all.\nSo on Cloud the branch is effectively DEAD CODE. It is latent, not absent. A self-hosted DC instance\nwith arbitrary screen configuration is exactly where it stops being latent.\n\nThat retires my earlier 'pre-existing, therefore follow-up' framing. Pre-existing was true and\nself-serving: it implied Cloud is unaffected when Cloud is merely lucky in its configuration.\n\nWHAT STRENGTHENS THIS TICKET: record_pending_key(local_id, jira_key) runs BEFORE the identity writes\n(:307-308), so the key is already durable when they fail. recover_pending's KEYED branch exists for\nprecisely this window and retro-attaches the label and property with NO search (binding_store.py:\n'Deterministic: the key is known ... No search.'). So the rollback delete is REDUNDANT with rebar's\nown recovery machinery — and worse than it, because deleting destroys created work while a\nkeyed-pending binding is visible and repairable.\n\nWHAT CUTS AGAINST THE RESEARCH'S RECOMMENDATION: 'stop deleting' is not obviously correct. On a\nPERMANENT failure the retro-attach fails again on every subsequent pass, so you trade a clean rollback\nfor a permanently-stuck pending binding. The real question is which failure is more recoverable, and\nthat is a design decision rather than a one-line change. Both options should be argued in the plan\nbefore implementation, with the permanent-vs-transient distinction explicit — _call_with_retry has\nalready removed the transient case, so this branch only ever sees permanent failures."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785509606481154001,"uuid":"a219c594-844c-444c-b247-cb971b86603a"}